HTML pages come from folders that contain `@entry.moth`. Routes come from the folder's position. No homepage is required.

### Directory routes

[codeblock, $code "text":
    @entry.moth          -> index.html
    about/@entry.moth    -> about/index.html
]

The root filename is fixed. Ordinary filenames such as `index.moth` have no page meaning. `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` publish APIs and emit no page.

### Entry rules

A folder becomes a page when it contains `@entry.moth`. An empty file or a metadata-only file still counts:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

Imported modules never run their entries.

`check` accepts a project with no entries. HTML `build` and `dev` need an entry somewhere below the selected project root, not necessarily at its root.

### Single files

When you select one file directly, an ordinary `.moth` file or `@entry.moth` becomes a page:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    moth build page.moth     -> page.html
    moth build @entry.moth   -> index.html
    moth build index.moth    -> index.html
]

A selected `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth` stays an API facade. `check` validates it. `build` reports that the project has no entry.

### Launch page

Development tooling opens the real homepage when the project has one. Otherwise it opens the first route in alphabetical order. It never invents a homepage. Requests to an empty site root stay missing.

The builder does not invent a homepage or scan arbitrary `.moth` files. Generated `_dev/` and `_release/` folders remain output, not source.
